title: axios (API testing)
section: Plugins
desc: Test HTTP APIs from bddr steps with axios: status codes as data, a call log in the report, auth, and options.
lede: The axios plugin gives steps an axios instance that is ready for testing. A 404 does not throw, so "the status is 404" is an ordinary assertion, and every call is recorded in the report.
---
<h2>Install</h2>
<aside class="callout note"><p class="callout-title">Not on npm yet</p><p>bddr is not published to the npm registry yet, so it is installed from your clone with <code>file:</code> paths. Once it is published, run <code>npm i -D @bddr/axios</code> plus the library instead, and this note will go away.</p></aside>
<div class="tabs" data-group="os">
  <section data-tab="macos" data-label="macOS">
<cb lang="bash">$ npm i -D file:../bddr/plugins/axios axios</cb>
  </section>
  <section data-tab="windows" data-label="Windows">
<cb lang="powershell">PS> npm i -D file:../bddr/plugins/axios axios</cb>
  </section>
  <section data-tab="linux" data-label="Linux">
<cb lang="bash">$ npm i -D file:../bddr/plugins/axios axios</cb>
  </section>
</div>

<h2>Configure</h2>
<cb title="bddr.config.ts">import { defineConfig } from '@bddr/cli';
import { axios } from '@bddr/axios';

export default defineConfig({
  features: 'features/**/*.feature',
  steps: 'steps/**/*.ts',
  plugins: [
    axios({
      baseURL: 'https://api.example.com',
      auth: { bearer: process.env.API_TOKEN },
      headers: { 'x-team': 'qa' },
    }),
  ],
});</cb>
<p>Add <code>import '@bddr/axios';</code> to one support file so <code>ctx.api</code>, <code>({ api })</code> and <code>inject('api')</code> are typed.</p>

<h2>Use it in steps</h2>
<cb lang="gherkin" title="features/products.feature">Scenario: Create a product
  When I create product "apple"
  Then the response status is 201

Scenario: Unknown product
  When I get product 9999
  Then the response status is 404</cb>
<cb title="steps/products.steps.ts">import { strict as assert } from 'node:assert';
import type { AxiosResponse } from 'axios';
import { When, Then } from '@bddr/runtime';

When('I create product {string}', async ({ api, state }, name: string) => {
  state.set('res', await api.post('/products', { name }));
});

When('I get product {int}', async ({ api, state }, id: number) => {
  state.set('res', await api.get(`/products/${id}`)); // a 404 does not throw
});

Then('the response status is {int}', ({ state }, status: number) => {
  assert.equal(state.get<AxiosResponse>('res').status, status);
});</cb>

<h2>What you get that plain axios does not give</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Status codes are data.</strong> 4xx and 5xx do not throw. Set <code>throwOnHttpError: true</code> to get axios's default back.</li>
  <li><strong>A call log in the report.</strong> Each call records method, final URL, status, duration and the request and response bodies. It is attached as <code>api-calls.json</code> when a scenario fails, or for every scenario with <code>attach: 'on'</code>. Network errors and timeouts are recorded too.</li>
  <li><strong>Masked secrets.</strong> Authorization, cookie and API-key headers appear as <code>***</code>. Bodies are cut at <code>maxBodyChars</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Credentials from the command line.</strong> <code>-D token=...</code> replaces the bearer token and <code>-D baseURL=...</code> the base URL.</li>
  <li><strong>No extra process.</strong> A scenario that only uses <code>api</code> starts nothing else.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="shot"><img src="../assets/img/axios-call-log.png" alt="A failed API scenario in the HTML report with its api-calls.json attachment expanded: method, URL, status, request headers with the token hidden, and the response body." width="2800" height="1800" loading="lazy"></figure>

<h2>Fixtures</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Scope</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>axios</code></td><td>worker</td><td>The <code>axios</code> module: <code>isAxiosError</code>, <code>create</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>api</code></td><td>scenario</td><td>An axios instance with the base URL, headers, timeout and auth from the options.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>newApi</code></td><td>scenario</td><td><code>newApi({ baseURL, headers })</code> gives another instance, for a second service or user. It records into the same call log.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Options</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>baseURL</code></td><td></td><td>API base URL. <code>-D baseURL=</code> overrides.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>headers</code></td><td></td><td>Default headers. <code>Accept: application/json</code> is added.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>timeoutMs</code></td><td><code>15000</code></td><td>Request timeout.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>auth</code></td><td></td><td><code>{ bearer }</code> (<code>-D token=</code> overrides) or <code>{ username, password }</code> for basic auth.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>throwOnHttpError</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Throw on 4xx and 5xx like axios.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>attach</code></td><td><code>'only-on-failure'</code></td><td><code>'off'</code>, <code>'on'</code> or <code>'only-on-failure'</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>maxBodyChars</code></td><td><code>5000</code></td><td>Longest body kept in the call log.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>config</code></td><td></td><td>Any other axios defaults: <code>proxy</code>, <code>httpsAgent</code>, <code>maxRedirects</code>.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The plugin uses your project's own <code>axios</code>, so <code>axios.isAxiosError(e)</code> in your steps and the plugin share one copy. <code>plugins/axios/example</code> runs offline with a local products API.</p>
